= Conclusion and Future Work <sec:concl>

== Conclusion <sec:concl-summary>

This report built and evaluated a two-layer coordinator for a small simulated warehouse fleet, in which a stall trigger calls a vision-language model on the overhead view and a deterministic layer turns the model's decision into robot commands.

*What was built.* The pipeline runs end to end in Isaac Sim with ROS 2, Nav2 and a hosted VLM: overhead perception with marker tracking, a stall trigger, the model call, deterministic grounding, phased dispatch and an execution layer that reports back, with Layer 2 switchable off by one key on identical code (Chapters 3 and 4). The same detect-to-dispatch chain also ran on physical TurtleBot3 robots, as a demonstration rather than an evaluation #ev("1.07"). The grounding layer checks every decision against the scene geometry, hands a yield to a robot that has room or refuses, and labels the provenance of every dispatch. A trace that re-grounds each recorded decision without its plan turns those labels into an audit of which executed commands came from the model #ev("6.02"). An independent safety monitor holds robot pairs apart and enforces speed limits, with a hard floor on centre distance in the live experiments #ev("1.04"); it does not check static obstacles. The live experiments were registered before their data, the post hoc analyses are labelled as post hoc #ev("1.05"), and the demonstration campaign was registered with its caps before any machine time was used, with every take, including every aborted one, archived and labelled #ev("5.51").

*What was shown.* In a simulated four-robot intersection jam, the full Layer 2 pipeline (congestion trigger, VLM call, grounding and phased dispatch) cleared the intersection in 4 of 5 episodes, against 0 of 5 with Layer 2 off. In a separate run, the live-VLM arm cleared it in 5 of 5 (4 of 4 without the smoke episode, whose acceptance deviated from the registered procedure) and a scripted arm that skipped the VLM call and ran the grounder's default plan in 4 of 4 valid episodes (5 of 5 after a post hoc re-run), in similar time. Both are exploratory pilots, n = 5 per arm, not independent samples, and never pooled #ev("6.05") #ev("6.06"). Gemini Robotics-ER 2 returned a valid, parseable decision in all 67 analyses of the demonstration campaign, but its plan was accepted unchanged in only 6 of 65 plan-channel analyses as recorded, 5 of them dispatched as written by an offline recomputation; in the intersection scene, the only one where this was computed, it changed the executed commands relative to the grounder's default once, by swapping the order of two near-equivalent back-out manoeuvres #ev("6.44") #ev("6.51") #ev("6.39"). In the two-robot head-on scenes, the grounded yielder was made correct by the grounder's feasibility gate, which overturned 37 of 79 model choices, not by the model, whose choice never beat the better of two trivial baselines #ev("6.08"). In pre-registered offline replays, no tested model met the pre-registered rule for choosing the yielder from the scene rather than from robot labels or listing order, and several cells could not be assessed #ev("6.09"). Safety evidence is limited to robot-pair centre distance; one sustained robot-obstacle contact, caused by an escort path that had no obstacle check, was confirmed offline from ground-truth poses and the recording #ev("6.42").

*Answer to the problem.* The question of @sec:intro-problem asked whether such a pipeline clears congestion that local reactive control leaves in place, and what each part contributes. In the intersection scene, the pipeline cleared a jam that Layer 1 alone never cleared, and the grounder's default plan carried that result without the model's plan. Beyond that scene, the registered five-robot pilot completed no episode in either arm, the convoy and five-robot demonstration takes stayed unresolved, and the head-on take resolved only after the gate had replaced the model's yielder #ev("6.14") #ev("6.41") #ev("6.42") #ev("6.37"). What the model supplied in these runs was a valid, structured decision on every call of the campaign: a strategy and a robot set, and, once, the order of two near-equivalent back-outs. Whether the strategy and robot set add anything over a scripted choice was not tested, because the scripted arm was handed them, and of the episodes in which the model's own decision fields changed execution, neither of the two that can be judged resolved #ev("6.02"). The plan that cleared the intersection came from the deterministic layer, and the grounded yielder in the head-on scenes was made correct by its feasibility gate. These results come from simulation, from small samples, and from one VLM in the live comparisons. Within those bounds, the report's lasting contribution is less a claim about the model than a way to measure its share: plan provenance per dispatch, a re-grounding without the plan, and a scripted default-plan arm on identical code.

== Future Work <sec:concl-future>

The limitations of @sec:disc-limits each suggest a next step. The most direct are these.

- *Test scene use properly.* Complete the improved-annotation replay, and run counterbalanced real frames for ER-2, with the listing order drawn at random in every live cell so that a slot prior can be separated from an identifier prior #ev("6.10") #ev("6.09").
- *Give plans a grammar the model can meet, and let verified plans do more.* Plans rarely survive the verifier, and the one switch that lets a verified plan act sets only the back-out order #ev("6.02") #ev("6.39"). A plan grammar that the model can satisfy, and a verified plan allowed to set more than the back-out order, would need a registered test against the default plan.
- *Make the plan follow through to dispatch and feedback.* `reverse` and `reroute` could be grounded from the plan, the visibility fix could be validated live, and the plan state shown back to the model could be corrected, which changes the prompt and therefore needs its own registered comparison #ev("6.51") #ev("6.80").
- *Recover latched clusters and tell the model about refusals.* The convoy latch needs a grounding that moves robots after it forms, and the model is not told when its decision was withheld #ev("6.40") #ev("6.15"). The per-cluster format of the offline bench is a candidate, but it has so far run only as a shadow that never dispatched #ev("6.47").
- *Check static obstacles on every straight manoeuvre.* The escort and reverse guards cover two manoeuvres. The recede and release legs of the intersection strategy and the backup and pull-off legs of a yield in declared geometry still have no sweep against declared boxes #ev("6.13"). A reverse could also be shortened to its clear length instead of refused, which would keep the manoeuvre but may not open the standoff #ev("6.13"). The margin rule of the escort guard still awaits a decision #ev("6.13").
- *Validate the safeguards live.* None of the escort guard, the reverse guard and the stall abort has fired live #ev("6.17") #ev("6.13") #ev("6.18"). A deterministic safety test, such as an authored box behind a robot that is told to back out, would show each of them acting, and would give the contact logger the live positive control it lacks #ev("6.17").
- *A live comparison after the fixes.* Run the Layer-2-on and Layer-2-off arms on the tranche-1 seed positions with the fixes keyed on. If a scripted default-plan arm is added, every M3 and M4 mechanism must be keyed identically in all arms that dispatch commands; the Layer-2-off arm dispatches none, so it is unaffected by construction #ev("6.60"). Such a run would be exploratory, carry its own tag, and never be pooled with the registered tranche. Running the registered tranche 2 would turn the pilot into the confirmatory test it was designed to be #ev("6.14").
- *Make the failure-cause rule decide more units.* The classification named no dominant cause and left 27 of 55 units undetermined (@sec:res-failure-causes). Recording what the reproduce check lacks would address the 15 units it leaves undetermined as written, a clock anchor in the Layer-2-off arm would make its 10 units codable, an order for codes within one class would settle the 2 tied units, and the unit that S1 links to an earlier escort needs a new rating #ev("6.63"). The interception counts say only what the mechanisms would have refused or aborted; whether scenes resolve with them keyed on, and how often the reverse guard refuses a leg that would have completed clear of every declared box, needs live runs #ev("6.63").
- *Widen the comparison.* A classical coordinator as a live arm, a second VLM in the live comparisons, and other layouts and robot counts would test what this report could not; RQ4 and RQ5 need experiments of their own.
